# app git-a2a

git-a2a is two things that ship together: a **standard** — one file, `a2amodule.yml`, at the
root of a git repository that describes the repository as a reusable module, names the agents
that own it, states how each of them wants to be contacted for each kind of request, and lists
the modules it depends on — and a **CLI**, `git-a2a`, that reads those files across repositories
and does the chores: fetches only the manifest of a dependency, wires the dependency into every
package manager the consuming repository uses, keeps a lock, and tells an agent who to ask and
how. It is the "service descriptor" of a micro-agent architecture: dependencies are taken on
agents at developer time, not on services at runtime.

The repository layout: `spec/` is the standard (format, semantics, examples) and is language
independent; `cmd/` and `internal/` are the Go implementation; `adapters/` holds one package
per ecosystem. Durable knowledge lives in `.hint` files next to what it governs; `plans/` is
local working material for whoever implements the next step and is never committed.

# goal A repository that can be imported with its owner, not only its code

A consumer runs one command against a git URL and gets three things at once: the dependency
wired into its own package managers (npm, uv, go.mod — whatever it actually uses), the
dependency's published surface and description where its agents can read them, and a resolved
answer to "who owns this and how do I ask them" for every kind of request. The owner of the
dependency wrote that down once, in its own repository, and git-a2a keeps it fresh.

# scope What git-a2a is and is not

In scope: the manifest standard and its lock; fetching manifests from git without cloning
repositories; wiring package-manager dependencies from the manifest; generating the agent-facing
summary into instruction files; resolving contacts per intent; validating and exporting A2A
agent cards; liveness/drift status.

Out of scope: running agents, hosting A2A endpoints, being a chat bridge, a registry service,
or a build system. git-a2a is stack-agnostic: it must work identically for a fleet built on
a chat-resident container fleet, on Claude Code subagents, on Copilot agents, or on a single
developer with a shell. Anything specific to one fleet tool is an example in `spec/examples/`, never a code path.

# decision One manifest file at the repository root, one lock next to it {#one_file}

The standard is `a2amodule.yml` at the repository root (a module may also live in a
subdirectory of a monorepo, in which case the file sits in that subdirectory and consumers
address it with `path:`). Its presence is the signal that a repository participates. All
sections — `module`, `agents`, `policy`, `dependencies` — live in that one file so that a
reader, a reviewer and `git diff` see the whole contract at once. `a2amodule.lock` records
what was resolved. Local state (fetched manifests, card snapshots, surface checkouts) lives in
`.git-a2a/` which is never committed; `git-a2a init` adds it to `.gitignore`.

Rationale: a folder with four files was considered and rejected — the parts share one lifecycle
and one reviewer, and a single file is what every adjacent convention (package.json,
pyproject.toml, MODULE.bazel) has taught the community to expect. Sections, not files, are the
unit of extension.

# decision The standard is language independent; the CLI is Go {#go}

The manifest standard (`spec/`) must never assume the implementation language. The reference
CLI is written in Go: one static binary with no runtime, installable where coding agents run
(containers, CI, laptops), and named `git-a2a` so that git itself exposes it as `git a2a …`.
Adapters shell out to the ecosystems' own tools (`yarn`, `uv`, `go`) rather than reimplementing
them, so the CLI's language is irrelevant to what it wires. Do not add a CGO dependency; do not
add a dependency for what the standard library does.

# decision Cross-platform builds and distribution are part of the first release {#distribution}

The first public release line is 1.0.x; the manifest schema remains independently versioned.
`CGO_ENABLED=0` and `-trimpath` make one Go source tree build for darwin/linux/windows ×
amd64/arm64 from any machine (`GOOS`/`GOARCH`), reproducibly. Releases are cut by goreleaser
from a tag: archives + checksums + SBOM on GitHub Releases, a Homebrew tap (`brew install
neprel/tap/git-a2a`), a Scoop/winget manifest for Windows, and — because agents and developers
already have `npx` and `uvx` — two thin wrappers: an npm package `git-a2a` whose per-platform
binaries ship as `optionalDependencies` (`@git-a2a/darwin-arm64`, …) the way esbuild, Biome and
Turborepo do, so `npx git-a2a` and `npm i -g git-a2a` just work; and a PyPI package `git-a2a`
with platform wheels carrying the binary so `uvx git-a2a` / `pipx install git-a2a` work. `go
install github.com/neprel/git-a2a/cmd/git-a2a@latest` and a one-line `curl | sh` installer
(checksum-verified) cover the rest; a Docker snippet documents adding the binary to an agent
image. All channels install the same binary with the same version string (`git-a2a --version`
prints version, commit, and build target). Wrappers contain no logic: they locate and exec the
binary, nothing else, so behaviour never depends on the channel.
The PyPI wheel builder embeds `dist/pypi/git_a2a.py` directly rather than maintaining a second
launcher string; on POSIX that launcher uses `os.execv` so the Go binary becomes the process.
Wheel ZIP entries use Unix creator metadata so the embedded binary retains its executable mode.

Learn from Caddy (Go, no npm/PyPI at all): its channels are the Go toolchain itself
(`go install github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/cmd/caddy@latest`, and `go run <module>@<version>`
is Go's own zero-install equivalent of `npx`/`uvx` — it works for git-a2a as
`go run github.com/neprel/git-a2a/cmd/git-a2a@latest`), static binaries on GitHub Releases, OS
package repositories (deb/rpm built with nfpm and hosted as apt/dnf repos — Caddy uses
Cloudsmith and Fedora COPR), Homebrew, Chocolatey/Scoop/winget, Docker images, a webi/curl
installer, and distro packages maintained by the distros. Priority for git-a2a therefore: (1)
GitHub Releases + `go install`/`go run` + curl installer — free with goreleaser and enough for
every CI and agent container that has Go or curl; (2) Homebrew tap, Scoop/winget, deb/rpm via
nfpm (goreleaser does it) with an apt/dnf repository when there are users to justify hosting;
(3) Docker image; (4) the npm and PyPI thin wrappers — a convenience for harnesses that have
node/python but neither Go nor curl, never a blocker for a release. Updating the tool: when a package manager installed it, that manager updates it — git-a2a
never writes over a binary it does not own; zero-install forms (`go run …@latest`, `npx`,
`uvx`) resolve per run and are pinned with `@version` in scripts; for the standalone binary
(curl installer, Releases download, baked into an image) an **explicit** `git-a2a upgrade
[--to X.Y.Z]` — like `caddy upgrade` — downloads the release for its platform, verifies the
checksum, replaces the file atomically, and prints the changelog. Explicit means on request,
never at start-up and never silently: a CLI that updates itself out of its pins breaks every
image that pinned it (a lesson already paid for). `git-a2a version [--check]` prints version,
commit, build target and the install channel (set by ldflags at build: brew, npm, pypi, deb,
rpm, binary, go), and with `--check` the latest release and the update command for *that*
channel; no network without `--check`. Agent images pin the version as a build ARG and bump it
deliberately. Verify the Caddy specifics against
https://caddyserver.com/docs/install when wiring the packaging.

# decision Public releases are published by GitHub Actions

The project is public and open source. Tag-triggered GitHub Actions runners build and publish
release artifacts and distribution wrappers; no paid or self-hosted release service is part of
the design. Third-party actions are pinned to full commit SHAs. `internal/version/VERSION` is
the single release-version source embedded by the CLI; the release workflow accepts `v` plus
that exact value or that value followed by a valid SemVer prerelease suffix, and rejects every
other tag before publishing. Build and tests pass before any publishing job.
On Windows a standalone-binary upgrade renames the running executable to an adjacent `.old`
file before installing its replacement; a failed install restores it, and the next invocation
removes the `.old` remnant best-effort.
Homebrew and Scoop publication is optional and skipped when its repository token is absent;
npm is likewise optional, while PyPI uses its protected GitHub environment and OIDC. winget is
not automated in the first release: add it only after a stable package identity and publisher
account exist. Prerelease tags are GitHub prereleases and never the `latest` release.
Release notes group user-visible conventional commits as features, fixes, performance,
refactoring, and documentation while omitting chore, CI, and test-only commits; their header
and footer explain the release, installation, checksums, and CLI reference. The GHCR image is
linked with `org.opencontainers.image.source`, becomes public after publication, and receives
`latest` only for a stable release.

# dep Reference CLI runtime dependencies

- `gopkg.in/yaml.v3` for YAML parsing and emission.

Everything else in the CLI uses the Go standard library or executes the user's installed
ecosystem tools. Add no other runtime dependency without recording and reviewing it here.

# decision Fetch the manifest, never the repository {#fetch_only_manifest}

`git-a2a add` and `update` obtain exactly `a2amodule.yml` (and, on demand, the declared
`surface` directory) from the remote. The resolution order is: `git ls-remote` to turn the ref
into a commit; then `git archive --remote` where the server supports it (plain ssh servers,
GitLab, Bitbucket); else a depth-1, blob-filtered, sparse clone into `.git-a2a/cache/`
(works on GitHub, GitLab, Gitea); else a plain shallow clone. Hosting REST APIs may be added as
an optional fast path, never as the only path — the standard must work against a bare ssh git
server. Measured 2026-08-21: sparse partial clone of one file from GitHub, 4 s and 152 KB;
`git archive --remote` on GitHub fails immediately with "Invalid command: git-upload-archive".
An omitted ref is resolved through `git ls-remote --symref ... HEAD` and stored as the remote's
actual default branch, not the moving token `HEAD`. Online status fetches reuse the dependency's
cached `.src` sparse checkout across runs.

# decision One resolved commit drives every ecosystem {#one_commit}

A dependency entry has one `ref`; `update` resolves it to one commit and records it in the lock;
every adapter then pins **that commit** in its ecosystem (`#commit=` for yarn, `#<sha>` for
npm/pnpm/bun, `rev =` for uv, a `replace … @<sha>` pseudo-version for Go). This is the default,
`track: locked`. The alternative the example repositories used — each ecosystem following
`main` on its own schedule — is exactly the failure git-a2a exists to remove: one change landed
in one language and not in another. `track: floating` is allowed per dependency and writes the
branch form for ecosystems that support it, but the lock still records the commit seen.

# decision Routing is intent → role → agent → contacts, and transport is open {#routing}

An agent entry lists `contacts`, each valid for one or more `intents` (question, change, bug,
review, incident, or any token), with an open `kind` (a2a, mattermost, discord, slack, telegram,
teams, email, github-issue, gitlab-issue, jira, url, …). The module's `policy.intents` maps each
intent to a role (owner, spec, maintainer, reviewer, support, …). Resolution for "I want to
ask X about Y" is: intent → role from policy (default owner) → agents with that role whose scope
matches → their contacts for that intent in declared order. git-a2a never chooses a transport
for the fleet; it reports the owner's declared preference and, in later phases, can deliver over
the kinds it has a driver for. A2A is the first-class kind because its card is the agent's
canonical self-description; it is not the only one.

# decision The agent card stays native A2A; the repository holds a binding, not a copy {#card_binding}

An agent's canonical description is its A2A Agent Card (spec v1.0, `/.well-known/agent-card.json`).
`a2amodule.yml` binds the repository to agents by name, role and scope and points at the card;
it does not restate the card. `git-a2a` snapshots cards into `.git-a2a/cache/` and records their
hash in the lock so drift is visible. `git-a2a card export` can emit a valid v1.0 card for an
agent that has an `a2a` contact, adding the git-a2a extension
`https://git-a2a.com/ext/module/v1` (params: module id, repository, role, scope) — extension is
the sanctioned way to add semantics to a card, and the A2A discovery text explicitly allows
cards delivered by "direct configuration" such as a file in a repository.
Legacy v0.3 cards are accepted as input, but card export upgrades every supported interface to
protocol version `1.0`; an exported v1.0 card never claims a v0.3 interface.

# decision Knowledge is asked for, unless the owner published it {#ask_not_read}

A consumer agent does not read a dependency's internals — not its code, not its `.hint`, not
its memory. It reads exactly what the owner published as `module.surface` and the fields of the
manifest, and for everything else it contacts the owning agent through the declared contacts.
git-a2a enforces the boundary by construction: it fetches the manifest and the surface and
nothing else. The owner is free to make the surface rich (generated from its own spec tooling)
precisely so that cheap questions never become paid agent turns.

# decision CLI discipline follows HINT {#cli_discipline}

stdout carries the requested data (human text by default, `--json` for machines); stderr carries
the verdict and advisories, decisive line first; exit 0 = ran successfully, 1 = a check ran and
found a failure (drift, unreachable agent, validation error), 2 = nothing resolved (unknown
dependency, no manifest, bad path). No command reports success over an empty subject set. Every
command that writes is idempotent and deterministic: same inputs, same bytes. Commands never read
the clock or randomness into anything they write; the lock carries commits and hashes, not
timestamps.

# decision Toolchains are prerequisites: detected and named, never installed {#prerequisites}

git-a2a wires dependencies by driving the ecosystems' own tools (`yarn`/`npm`/`pnpm`/`bun`,
`uv`/`pip`, `go`, `cargo`, `swift`, `dart`, `bundle`, `composer`, `mix`, `cabal`/`stack`, `zig`,
`clj`, `nix`) and git itself (≥ 2.25 for sparse-checkout, ≥ 2.27 preferred for partial clone).
It never installs, downloads or upgrades any of them — that is the package manager's or the
operator's job, and a tool that installs toolchains is a supply-chain surface nobody asked for.
What it does instead: `git-a2a doctor` lists, for the ecosystems detected in this repository and
for every wired dependency, which tool is required, whether it is on PATH and its version, and
prints the one-line install hint for the platform; every adapter checks for its tool before
`Refresh` and fails with the same actionable line (`pypi: uv not found — install: …`) instead of
a raw exec error; `status -v` shows `tool missing` per ecosystem; wiring the manifest entry (a
text edit) never requires the tool, so `add` succeeds and says what is missing for `Refresh`.
Missing tools are a warning on `add`/`wire` and a failure only for an explicitly requested
`Refresh`/`--sync`.

# invariant Generated blocks are owned by git-a2a and nothing else {#managed_blocks}

Everything git-a2a writes into a file it does not own (AGENTS.md, package.json, pyproject.toml,
go.mod, .gitignore) is either a delimited managed block (`<!-- git-a2a:begin -->` …
`<!-- git-a2a:end -->` or the ecosystem's own structure) or a precisely addressed entry. A
re-run replaces only that; a human edit outside it survives; a human edit inside it is
overwritten and `status` says so beforehand. Never rewrite a whole file to change one entry.
When npm has a package lock, refresh runs `npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts`
(with audit and funding network calls disabled), never a bare lifecycle-capable install.

# invariant The lock is the source of truth for what was resolved {#lock_truth}

`a2amodule.lock` is written only by git-a2a, is deterministic, and records for every dependency
the git URL, the ref, the resolved commit, the hash of the fetched manifest and the hash of each
snapshotted card. Adapters read the commit from the lock; `status` compares the lock against the
remote and against what the ecosystems actually pin. Ecosystem lockfiles are downstream.

# invariant Text that comes from a dependency is data, never instruction {#untrusted_text}

Every string git-a2a renders from another repository's manifest or surface (descriptions,
contact notes, policy notes, surface listings) is untrusted input that will land in an agent's
context. Rendered output therefore: labels it with its origin ("from acme-lib-utils
@<commit>"), places it inside the managed block as quoted data and never as an imperative line
addressed to the reader, caps its length (`description` ≤ 600 characters, `note` ≤ 300, the
rest truncated with a marker), strips control characters and HTML comments (so a dependency
cannot close or open a managed block), and `update` shows the textual diff of what changed
before it is rendered (`--review`, default on when a TTY is attached). The CLI never executes
anything a manifest names — no scripts, no URLs fetched beyond cards and git.

# rule Unknown is not an error where the standard says it is open {#open_vocabularies}

`contacts[].kind`, `intents`, `agents[].role` and `exports[].ecosystem` are open vocabularies: a
value the CLI does not know is carried through verbatim, shown to the user, and never rejected by
`validate`. Unknown **keys** in a known object are errors, except keys prefixed `x-`, which are
reserved for extensions and preserved byte-for-byte.

# bad Things that must not creep in

* A second description of an agent that can disagree with its card. The manifest binds; the
  card describes.
* A git-a2a runtime process, daemon, registry or database. It is a CLI over files in git.
* Cloning a dependency to read one file.
* A default that is specific to one fleet tool, chat platform or coding harness. Those are
  examples under `spec/examples/`.
* Timestamps or machine paths in anything committed.
* Shelling out to a package manager with `--force` or with flags that rewrite files beyond the
  one entry being wired.

# build

`go build ./...` and `go test ./...` are the gate. Tests include: golden tests for every
adapter on fixture repositories under `testdata/` (the `acme-lib-utils` / `acme-app-cli` pair);
determinism (render twice, compare bytes); an end-to-end test against local bare repositories
created by the test (`file://` URLs, `uploadpack.allowFilter=true` set on the fixture) so no
network is required; schema validation of every file under `spec/examples/`. `hint status` is
run in CI as an advisory. Verification also includes a ten-manifest public corpus per ecosystem,
spec/schema/Go-field parity, Windows unit tests, weekly/manual real-toolchain matrices, opt-in
real-host fetch tests, and the pinned A2A-proto conformance script. Prerequisite tests use a fake
`PATH`; they must prove `doctor` fails clearly while wiring remains available without installing
or downloading a toolchain.
